# Strike acid blobs with an ORDINARY long sword and read the sword's own
# description page before and after (bd inc-m2zi).
#
#   INCURSION_OPTIONS=tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat \
#       tools/headless.sh tools/keys/dequ-hardness-plain.keys 5
#
# WHAT IS BEING MEASURED. The acid blob's A_DEQU is 2d4 AD_ACID and no longer
# carries a save DC (lib/mon3.irh:2585). An ordinary iron weapon has hardness
# 10 and 2d4 cannot exceed 8, so before this change the blob could never
# scratch one. A non-magical item now has its hardness bypassed and takes the
# whole roll.
#
# THE ORACLE is the item's own page, reached from the inventory by selecting
# the Weapon Hand ('b') and pressing 'x'. It states the wear in its title and
# the hit points in the Composition paragraph:
#
#     The Uncursed Long Sword ('(')            It has 15 out of 15 hit points.
#     The Partly Melted Uncursed Long Sword    It has  9 out of 15 hit points.
#
# ONE BLOB PER BLOW, deliberately, for two reasons. A blow that KILLS its
# target leaves no item for the retaliation to aim at -- the engine aims it at
# the striker instead and prints "Acid splashes you!" -- and a blow into an
# empty square would walk the character east and spend every later keystroke on
# the wrong thing. Summoning a fresh blob before each blow keeps the character
# still and gives every blow a living target. Three blows are struck because
# whether any one blow kills its blob depends on the blob's hit dice.
#
# THE COMPANION SCRIPT is tools/keys/dequ-hardness-magic.keys, which does the
# same thing with a MAGICAL sword, whose hardness is not bypassed. They are two
# scripts and two sessions rather than two halves of one, because the
# retaliations that land on the character rather than on his sword cost him
# 2d4 hit points each and a level-one orc cannot pay for both halves.

@include dequ-setup.keys

w
@choose "Item Acquisition"
RIGHT*9                 # walk the glyph row to ordinary weapons
@until "Long sword" DOWN
DOWN                    # the wanted row is the middle of three; step onto it
ENTER
b                       # Weapon Hand -- the slot lettering skips 'd' (Drop)
SPACE                   # swap the sword into it
@while "two-handed" n
s                       # stow the starting knife the swap pushed into the air
@until "SPACE:Swap" i    # the inventory manager, if it is not already open
@until ">>b)Weapon" b    # select the Weapon Hand
@until "base value of" x # Examine: the item's own description page
@dump:plain-before
@until "Things in View" ESC

# OPT_WARN_DEQU asks before a blow that risks the weapon, so answer it when it
# is asked. It is asked here and not in the magical run, which is itself a
# consequence of the same rule.
w
@choose "Monster Summoning"
@choose "Jellies"
@choose "acid blob"
RIGHT ENTER
RIGHT
@while "Attack the" y
@dump:plain-strike-1

w
@choose "Monster Summoning"
@choose "Jellies"
@choose "acid blob"
RIGHT ENTER
RIGHT
@while "Attack the" y
@dump:plain-strike-2

w
@choose "Monster Summoning"
@choose "Jellies"
@choose "acid blob"
RIGHT ENTER
RIGHT
@while "Attack the" y
@dump:plain-strike-3

@until "SPACE:Swap" i
@until ">>b)Weapon" b
@until "base value of" x
@dump:plain-after
@until "Things in View" ESC
@quit
